      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To investigate the prevalence of back pain in a general population aged between 20 and 59 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POPULATION",
          "content": "A representative sample of 2000 individuals from Ostergotland County, Sweden (population 400,000)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross-sectional study using a questionnaire including the pain drawing."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The observed point prevalence was 28% (95% confidence interval 26-31%). The adjusted prevalence taking into account the non-responders was 23% (21-25%). Lumbar pain with radiation was reported by 40%, while 4% had only cervical pain with radiation. Twelve per cent were on sick-leave due to back pain. Activity of daily life was affected mainly in the group of men aged 40-59 and only in household tasks. The back problems did not affect social activity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The prevalence of back problems in the vocational ages was found to be 23%. Only small parts of a pain population are on sick-leave or have changed working tasks because of back problems. The distribution of pain in most cases is combined with radiation to extremities and not isolated to a single region. The combination of different localisations shows the pain problem to be more than just a \"low back\" problem."
        }
      ]},
